1
00:00:01,117 --> 00:00:03,158
[SPEAKER_02]: What does it mean to endure things rightly?

2
00:00:03,879 --> 00:00:13,485
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it just about patience and gritting one's teeth within the framework of the precepts, or is there more to it?

3
00:00:13,525 --> 00:00:14,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's how it starts.

4
00:00:16,827 --> 00:00:19,629
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you certainly shouldn't be trying to...

5
00:00:20,710 --> 00:00:29,155
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're just starting out, you haven't been keeping the precepts, you don't really have anything to endure except the discomfort of not breaking the precepts.

6
00:00:34,566 --> 00:00:36,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, it is more than that.

7
00:00:38,527 --> 00:00:49,011
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not just waiting, as if simply not doing certain things externally is going to tame your mind.

8
00:00:49,071 --> 00:00:50,491
[SPEAKER_01]: It may not even be uncomfortable.

9
00:00:50,511 --> 00:00:52,932
[SPEAKER_01]: You will get used to it pretty soon.

10
00:00:54,663 --> 00:00:56,444
[SPEAKER_01]: As most monks do.

11
00:00:57,165 --> 00:01:01,068
[SPEAKER_01]: And it doesn't even mean that they're practicing.

12
00:01:01,628 --> 00:01:12,136
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's... What you have to endure is much more than just random pains as well.

13
00:01:12,296 --> 00:01:17,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you're not just seeking out things to to make yourself experience pain.

14
00:01:18,201 --> 00:01:23,685
[SPEAKER_01]: It's when the mind craves for something and you don't provide it,

15
00:01:26,245 --> 00:01:28,546
[SPEAKER_01]: When pain comes up, that's what you endure.

16
00:01:28,627 --> 00:01:29,907
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not even about the pain.

17
00:01:31,548 --> 00:01:33,269
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not even that the pain is good or something.

18
00:01:33,690 --> 00:01:38,112
[SPEAKER_01]: It simply is a symptom of the fact that you're not giving in to something unwholesome.

19
00:01:40,014 --> 00:01:48,339
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you, by enduring the pain, like, unless you can endure the pain, you will cease to restrain that unwholesome thing.

20
00:01:49,897 --> 00:01:51,877
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's really what the emphasis should be in.

21
00:01:52,037 --> 00:01:58,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I restraining the unwholesome intention that arose, whether or not it's painful?

22
00:01:58,359 --> 00:01:59,839
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if it's painful, I endure it.

23
00:02:00,859 --> 00:02:03,000
[SPEAKER_01]: But sometimes, it may not even be painful.

24
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,900
[SPEAKER_01]: In fact, eventually, it would be pleasant.

25
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:16,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then, the mind starts to understand that not going for those things is actually the better alternative.

26
00:02:19,071 --> 00:02:23,913
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in terms of, like, how do you do that then?

27
00:02:23,933 --> 00:02:28,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, how do you know that you're actually restraining an unwholesome intention?

28
00:02:28,735 --> 00:02:43,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, precisely by not putting the emphasis on whether it's painful or pleasant to... like, whether the situation is painful or pleasant is not to measure.

29
00:02:44,747 --> 00:03:02,101
[SPEAKER_01]: It could be extremely painful to not do something and then it's actually bad, like you're actually going the wrong way by avoiding that thing, like all the self-mortification practices that you would read about in the suttas.

30
00:03:03,122 --> 00:03:03,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

31
00:03:03,322 --> 00:03:10,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Because those things are imposed independently of discerning the mind's craving.

32
00:03:13,205 --> 00:03:24,735
[SPEAKER_01]: They just abstractly reasoned as, oh, well, if I renounce more things, then I am closer to freedom from attachments or something.

33
00:03:25,095 --> 00:03:31,480
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you want to see is, what are the things that my mind is actually leaning towards?

34
00:03:32,261 --> 00:03:40,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Meaning, kind of the things that I kind of find myself being being pushed

35
00:03:42,467 --> 00:03:49,108
[SPEAKER_01]: To engage in these things, whether I like it or not, and then I can, you know, secondarily say yes or say no.

36
00:03:49,848 --> 00:03:54,729
[SPEAKER_01]: But, fundamentally, that impulse was there first.

37
00:03:57,250 --> 00:04:01,190
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those are the things you want to start investigating and seeing.

38
00:04:02,351 --> 00:04:09,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, and you will see, naturally, when you don't follow those impulses that came on their own, but you actually discern them,

39
00:04:12,045 --> 00:04:15,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then, that's the only way that you will be restraining them.

40
00:04:15,386 --> 00:04:20,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you're just doing random observances externally.

41
00:04:20,789 --> 00:04:22,190
[SPEAKER_01]: But you actually see those impulses.

42
00:04:22,830 --> 00:04:23,730
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't give in to them.

43
00:04:25,131 --> 00:04:27,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, that discomfort is going to come up.

44
00:04:30,174 --> 00:04:31,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Strongly or weakly, it doesn't matter.

45
00:04:31,854 --> 00:04:34,216
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is, you didn't give in.

46
00:04:38,498 --> 00:04:38,758
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

47
00:04:40,735 --> 00:04:42,398
[SPEAKER_02]: That happens with the precepts, obviously.

48
00:04:43,700 --> 00:04:44,261
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just like...

49
00:04:46,702 --> 00:04:57,106
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a bit simpler in the sense that you don't really have to question your intentions as much, especially in the beginning, because it's pretty clear what you shouldn't do.

50
00:04:57,166 --> 00:05:07,209
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for somebody who's who's not used to even that, then it's gonna... just that blind keeping the precepts is already gonna involve restraining their intentions.

51
00:05:07,890 --> 00:05:14,092
[SPEAKER_01]: But at some point, you're not even gonna have intentions to break the precepts, but you're gonna have other intentions.

52
00:05:15,093 --> 00:05:16,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Still based on craving.

53
00:05:17,576 --> 00:05:22,683
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're not making effort to discern them, then nothing is going to show you that they're unwholesome.

54
00:05:23,284 --> 00:05:29,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not even that you're going to be feeding this massive dukkha on account of them that would show you that there's a problem.

55
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:54,002
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's when you would have to start seeing, like, well, regardless of whether it's externally prohibited or regardless of even whether it feels painful when I engage in it, I have to start putting first the fact that, well, there was an impulse there.

56
00:05:57,577 --> 00:06:23,365
[SPEAKER_01]: And the going along with those impulses, in and of itself, keeps you within saṃsāra, because it means, you know, you could have survived that one episode, could have been fine, nothing bad happened, but you are training your mind, you're training yourself, so that when things come up painful or pleasant, you have to act.

57
00:06:27,070 --> 00:06:31,072
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's going to be a matter of time until that...

58
00:06:31,172 --> 00:06:53,762
[SPEAKER_01]: In fact, well, the mere... if you were to, let's say, see in hindsight, the mere fact of needing to act, even if there are absolutely no negative consequences on account of it externally, the mere fact of needing to act, that impulse, is already painful.

59
00:06:54,422 --> 00:06:55,943
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already a burden that you're carrying.

60
00:06:57,302 --> 00:07:00,507
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, you've been carrying that forever, so you don't realize it.

61
00:07:02,471 --> 00:07:10,965
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's an issue, whatever kind of pressure it is, to still be giving into it, because you're you're training that habit.

62
00:07:14,768 --> 00:07:20,353
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact that it doesn't seem like an issue is actually exactly how it goes on indefinitely.

63
00:07:20,413 --> 00:07:25,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody just wakes up and abandons existence.

64
00:07:26,778 --> 00:07:28,860
[SPEAKER_01]: You just get caught up in it one way or the other.

65
00:07:34,608 --> 00:07:45,756
[SPEAKER_02]: So, like, the enduring things rightly is less about, let's say, finding the right method or technique or way of enduring.

66
00:07:48,183 --> 00:07:55,048
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's more the discernment and clarity towards what I'm actually enduring.

67
00:07:56,148 --> 00:08:02,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, but even then, like, it's... again, the point is not what... the point is not the enduring.

68
00:08:02,593 --> 00:08:12,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the enduring is a side effect of not giving in to something unwholesome.

69
00:08:13,320 --> 00:08:17,223
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's important because if you put first the enduring,

70
00:08:18,865 --> 00:08:44,972
[SPEAKER_01]: You might ignore that that very effort that you're putting into endure this is, let's say, a type of restlessness, like subjecting yourself to painful circumstances out of some impatient attitudes towards the practice, like you want to speed it up or something, and then you go and start fasting or whatever out of that intention.

71
00:08:45,432 --> 00:08:45,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Not that it's

72
00:08:47,576 --> 00:08:54,477
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not inherently bad to experience difficulties, and you should always try to go the comfortable route.

73
00:08:54,678 --> 00:08:54,838
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

74
00:08:55,118 --> 00:08:57,858
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is always, why were you going towards that?

75
00:08:59,058 --> 00:09:10,461
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, then you would be overlooking what actually matters, if you just think that enduring discomfort is inherently part of the training.

76
00:09:11,321 --> 00:09:15,602
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, enduring discomfort is what goes hand in hand

77
00:09:17,268 --> 00:09:20,951
[SPEAKER_01]: With not giving in to unwholesome states.

78
00:09:22,552 --> 00:09:27,195
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't mean that whenever you're enduring discomfort, you're actually not giving into something unwholesome.

79
00:09:28,797 --> 00:09:40,225
[SPEAKER_01]: In fact, you could even be enduring discomfort on account of, like, I don't know, having given into sensual things, like enduring the discomfort of a marriage or having kids.

80
00:09:44,115 --> 00:09:52,699
[SPEAKER_02]: But it will feel... there will always be discomfort if I restrain myself from giving in to the impulses.

81
00:09:52,959 --> 00:09:53,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

82
00:09:54,420 --> 00:09:59,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, it doesn't have to be super coarse and like you're falling apart or something.

83
00:10:00,602 --> 00:10:02,703
[SPEAKER_01]: There's just... there's a uneasiness.

84
00:10:04,824 --> 00:10:09,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it would have felt easier to just go with it.

85
00:10:10,587 --> 00:10:12,828
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's pretty much the definition of what an impulse is.

86
00:10:19,865 --> 00:10:42,591
[SPEAKER_02]: So, there would be the difference as well between... like, wrongly enduring would be still doing the endurance as a form of activity, whereas rightly enduring is, like you said, the result of discerning and having clarity about, fundamentally, what's wholesome, what's unwholesome.

87
00:10:42,651 --> 00:10:43,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

88
00:10:43,171 --> 00:10:43,751
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a duty.

89
00:10:43,791 --> 00:10:47,072
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes a duty you take up, which is blinding.

90
00:10:47,112 --> 00:10:49,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I do this because it is good by itself.

91
00:10:51,283 --> 00:10:58,032
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, if it stops being unpleasant, then you feel like, okay, so what do I do now?

92
00:11:00,055 --> 00:11:02,919
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's like... that wasn't the point.

93
00:11:17,682 --> 00:11:21,246
[SPEAKER_02]: And you mentioned speeding up the practice.

94
00:11:24,028 --> 00:11:34,678
[SPEAKER_02]: Are there any ways or legitimate, yeah, reasons, I guess, for actually speeding up the practice?

95
00:11:35,099 --> 00:11:38,963
[SPEAKER_02]: Or what would be a way to speed up the practice, if there is a way?

96
00:11:40,572 --> 00:11:44,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it can never be anything you do blindly, that's for sure.

97
00:11:45,635 --> 00:11:56,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it can never be just a technique or method that you do and you just apply it verbatim and then that makes it work by itself.

98
00:11:56,962 --> 00:12:04,567
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, like, you follow this procedure and the product is going to be assembled and ready just because you follow the procedure.

99
00:12:05,927 --> 00:12:12,150
[SPEAKER_01]: It never works that way, because the entire training, the... well, suffering as well, is all based on your intentions.

100
00:12:13,751 --> 00:12:20,734
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cannot fix the issue without actually addressing the root cause.

101
00:12:21,195 --> 00:12:26,157
[SPEAKER_01]: And, the way to speed up the practice, actually,

102
00:12:28,305 --> 00:12:38,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, look for what are the things that you are not actually restraining, that are impulses.

103
00:12:41,572 --> 00:12:50,396
[SPEAKER_01]: So, quite often, actually, you would naturally gravitate to picking up these random observances.

104
00:12:52,257 --> 00:13:05,193
[SPEAKER_01]: As like, okay, well, if I stop eating with a spoon, I will... because you need... that's more uncomfortable and surely, like, that's gonna...

105
00:13:07,815 --> 00:13:12,357
[SPEAKER_01]: It's preferable to eat with a spoon versus not eating with a spoon.

106
00:13:12,377 --> 00:13:15,298
[SPEAKER_01]: It's preferable to wear clothes rather than being naked.

107
00:13:16,538 --> 00:13:20,320
[SPEAKER_01]: It's preferable to eat normal food versus just eating leaves.

108
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:26,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, surely, I am giving up craving because I am going for this more difficult thing.

109
00:13:28,022 --> 00:13:29,583
[SPEAKER_01]: But was there an impulse there?

110
00:13:31,858 --> 00:13:38,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that you were actually, like, were you obsessed about wearing clothes and eating with a spoon?

111
00:13:40,651 --> 00:13:45,758
[SPEAKER_02]: Or do you just assume that because it's more difficult, it's going to be training your mind?

112
00:13:46,833 --> 00:13:53,217
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, ironically, simply because you have that view, you have impulses that you will completely overlook and give in to.

113
00:13:53,997 --> 00:14:08,786
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what the Buddha and the suttas... you would see these criticisms of these ascetics back in the day, that they would be doing all these inspiring ascetic practices,

114
00:14:10,167 --> 00:14:28,117
[SPEAKER_01]: But then they would, like, when whenever a monk would arrive, like a Buddhist monk, to see them, it would be like just absolutely making a racket, arguing about their views, you know, like just behaving like just unrestrained, really.

115
00:14:29,998 --> 00:14:36,142
[SPEAKER_01]: And obviously, it doesn't have to be that way in order to still, you know, it could be small things.

116
00:14:38,177 --> 00:14:40,418
[SPEAKER_01]: That nobody would blame you for, externally.

117
00:14:40,498 --> 00:14:44,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody would think, oh, look, he's greedy, or he's averse.

118
00:14:45,620 --> 00:14:59,925
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were honest, you would say, oh, yeah, I do have an impulse for this, that I'm not really able to... Like, if I didn't give in to it, I would be subject to rebellion from my own mind.

119
00:15:03,511 --> 00:15:11,513
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those are the things that you have to... it really comes down to honesty, to recognizing those things.

120
00:15:11,673 --> 00:15:16,475
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that's how you will speed up your practice, like not making exceptions on that level.

121
00:15:17,956 --> 00:15:30,920
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, almost like very rarely would it actually be something very straightforward, where you could just decide, oh, I'm not going to do X and that's it.

122
00:15:32,691 --> 00:15:34,472
[SPEAKER_02]: It's far more subtle than that most of the time.

123
00:15:34,672 --> 00:15:51,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's a specific kind of habits and avenues of sort of finding safety that you might have, making things the way you prefer.

124
00:15:53,537 --> 00:16:01,539
[SPEAKER_01]: And the problem is like, if you don't have enough honesty, you won't even see them, because it's just like... it just goes through.

125
00:16:02,883 --> 00:16:04,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Automatically accepted.

126
00:16:05,064 --> 00:16:12,546
[SPEAKER_02]: And they're probably not too big of a deal as well, that people you would live with would point it out to you, and would say, well, it's not okay to behave like that.

127
00:16:12,586 --> 00:16:12,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

128
00:16:13,486 --> 00:16:15,587
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not even necessarily the same thing all the time.

129
00:16:15,607 --> 00:16:21,329
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like every single day I'm doing it, and like, it's just obvious.

130
00:16:22,089 --> 00:16:27,190
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you have to learn to pick up the signs of your mind, as Siddha described.

131
00:16:27,310 --> 00:16:32,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the same way as the the king doesn't have the same preferences from day to day,

132
00:16:33,753 --> 00:16:37,016
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't even have the same impulses from day to day.

133
00:16:37,336 --> 00:16:38,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Initially, you will.

134
00:16:40,279 --> 00:16:50,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Sexuality and these very coarse things, you can just pinpoint them and be sure, like, okay, I'm still struggling with this, but eventually, it's going to be kind of so...

135
00:16:52,867 --> 00:16:53,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Indirect.

136
00:16:53,787 --> 00:16:58,168
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, not necessarily like hidden, but just personal.

137
00:16:58,228 --> 00:17:03,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, personal or kind of circumstantial as well.

138
00:17:03,749 --> 00:17:06,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Like it's not even going to come up every day.

139
00:17:06,350 --> 00:17:17,892
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were honest, you would know in this specific scenario, which I may never have experienced before, this way of acting is going along with the impulses.

140
00:17:21,768 --> 00:17:32,074
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it would be... like, obviously, speeding up the practice usually comes along with, okay, I'm going to pile these additional activities, additional observances on top of each other.

141
00:17:32,094 --> 00:17:32,234
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

142
00:17:32,254 --> 00:17:33,334
[SPEAKER_01]: So, restraining that as well.

143
00:17:34,055 --> 00:17:37,216
[SPEAKER_01]: That's that's going to be part of speeding up your practice.

144
00:17:37,336 --> 00:17:40,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, not acting out of... See, that's a great example, actually.

145
00:17:41,639 --> 00:17:47,882
[SPEAKER_01]: These these restless impulses that you would have to to just pile up more things.

146
00:17:50,649 --> 00:17:53,011
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if, again, it feels completely justified.

147
00:17:53,411 --> 00:17:55,013
[SPEAKER_02]: Even mentally contemplating.

148
00:17:55,033 --> 00:17:55,933
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, exactly.

149
00:17:55,953 --> 00:18:01,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, just adding, like, oh, I've spent three hours every day contemplating such and such.

150
00:18:04,300 --> 00:18:08,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, I read in the suttas that this leads to arhantship.

151
00:18:08,163 --> 00:18:14,408
[SPEAKER_01]: So I'm just gonna... And then, yeah, sure, somebody might have dedicated themselves to contemplating that.

152
00:18:14,448 --> 00:18:16,510
[SPEAKER_01]: But are you doing it out of an impulse?

153
00:18:19,345 --> 00:18:26,873
[SPEAKER_01]: In which case, you certainly are not contemplating whatever it is that was actually being referred to there.

154
00:18:31,397 --> 00:18:38,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whatever it is that is actually wholesome, is along the lines of subduing impulses.

155
00:18:43,575 --> 00:18:47,676
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's also why the practice really begins with the right view.

156
00:18:47,736 --> 00:18:54,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when you have this reference point to know, oh, this is actually an impulse.

157
00:18:55,539 --> 00:19:12,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you will be able to include craving, actually, rather than just kind of blindly accepting it, when it camouflages itself into these kind of supposed wholesome things.

158
00:19:15,947 --> 00:19:23,651
[SPEAKER_02]: It would be like a practical way of going about it, given that you are in an environment that's, you know...

159
00:19:25,080 --> 00:19:35,405
[SPEAKER_02]: Where there isn't any kind of course obstructions that you... You have certain activities that are just on a day-to-day basis you need to do to maintain the place, to maintain yourself.

160
00:19:35,525 --> 00:19:41,987
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, you need to eat, you need to clean a few things, probably even as a lay person, if you kind of live on your own or family.

161
00:19:43,168 --> 00:19:47,070
[SPEAKER_02]: But you have certain activities throughout the day that are necessary in a way.

162
00:19:48,050 --> 00:19:55,517
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, then, there might be days, it's raining, you cannot clean, and then you will actually might notice, oh, actually, there's this restlessness, and then did the cleaning all.

163
00:19:55,537 --> 00:19:57,559
[SPEAKER_02]: But, let's say, you have these basic activities.

164
00:19:57,839 --> 00:19:57,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

165
00:19:58,019 --> 00:20:02,663
[SPEAKER_01]: But, exactly, but that doesn't mean, that doesn't mean that you were always cleaning out of that restlessness.

166
00:20:02,703 --> 00:20:03,164
[SPEAKER_01]: It depends.

167
00:20:03,264 --> 00:20:03,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

168
00:20:04,024 --> 00:20:08,028
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, that's what I meant just now with, like, situation just come up.

169
00:20:09,610 --> 00:20:13,196
[SPEAKER_01]: And it doesn't necessarily have to have any connection with anything that happened before.

170
00:20:14,097 --> 00:20:22,812
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, it could be that you just, right now, have an impulse to get rid of that lack of activity somehow.

171
00:20:24,136 --> 00:20:42,388
[SPEAKER_01]: And you need to see it there and then, as like, okay, this impulse has arisen now, rather than sort of solidify it too much into like, okay, so I'm just like doing too much, so let me take on an observance of reducing my activities and just doing less.

172
00:20:45,227 --> 00:20:55,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Because even if, sometimes, you were doing the cleaning out of restlessness, the fact that you just imposed it in that blanket way means you don't know what you're doing.

173
00:20:55,531 --> 00:20:57,993
[SPEAKER_01]: So, some other impulse is going to come up and you're not going to see it.

174
00:21:00,774 --> 00:21:08,397
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just moving from one thing to the other, basically, like a... kind of like a... like a shadow, like, you know, wherever you cast the light, now the shadow is elsewhere.

175
00:21:17,579 --> 00:21:18,620
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't have to be that way.

176
00:21:18,640 --> 00:21:32,210
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like it's inherently kind of arcane and has so many moving parts that it's just... that's why it's so hard to succeed in training your mind.

177
00:21:33,231 --> 00:21:45,941
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that kind of tendency to prioritize things that are secondary and not want to look at the often very simple fact that, yes, there is an impulse here.

178
00:21:47,515 --> 00:22:01,848
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you kind of have a tendency to just do anything other than sort of stay with that ambiguous level of, oh, I see that there's just this pressure towards this.

179
00:22:02,149 --> 00:22:04,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not even necessarily towards a specific thing.

180
00:22:06,533 --> 00:22:07,654
[SPEAKER_01]: It just arose that way.

181
00:22:08,855 --> 00:22:13,059
[SPEAKER_01]: And it kind of looks for gratification here and there.

182
00:22:16,294 --> 00:22:19,757
[SPEAKER_01]: And just seeing that as the issue, like seeing, acting out of that as the issue.

183
00:22:20,658 --> 00:22:27,243
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, oh, but how many times a day I end up cleaning, or how many hours a day I spend cleaning.

184
00:22:27,263 --> 00:22:31,647
[SPEAKER_01]: It's much easier to just, kind of, because then you can, you can...

185
00:22:33,208 --> 00:22:40,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Get a lot more certainty and say, okay, so now, from now onwards, if I just limit my cleaning to one hour a day, I'll be fine.

186
00:22:40,913 --> 00:22:44,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then, you won't have to think about it next time.

187
00:22:44,115 --> 00:22:46,336
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't have to see what impulse is there next time.

188
00:22:48,058 --> 00:22:50,299
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's kind of like, everybody has that.

189
00:22:50,339 --> 00:22:52,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you already want to know what you're going to do ahead.

190
00:22:54,342 --> 00:22:55,542
[SPEAKER_01]: And it just doesn't work that way.

191
00:22:56,063 --> 00:22:59,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the the impulses, the direction of the impulses, is going to change.

192
00:23:03,382 --> 00:23:08,666
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like you have a fog, such that you can only see ahead of you, like, one meter.

193
00:23:09,947 --> 00:23:10,987
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have to accept that.

194
00:23:12,569 --> 00:23:19,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, whatever map do you think you have of what's gonna come after, it's not... it's wrong.

195
00:23:21,135 --> 00:23:24,177
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to make decisions based on, like, what's right ahead of you.

196
00:23:33,837 --> 00:23:37,279
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like your sense of urgency is getting ignored.

197
00:23:38,760 --> 00:23:40,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's a misdirected sense of urgency.

198
00:23:42,142 --> 00:23:56,491
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because the sense of urgency leads to, like, effort, faith, all of those are factors of awakening or things that lead to awakening if you have the right view.

199
00:23:56,631 --> 00:23:58,832
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you know what it is that you have to do.

200
00:24:02,216 --> 00:24:04,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, sense of urgency can be...

201
00:24:04,180 --> 00:24:12,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Imagine, like, how many wrong views you could pick up with a sense of urgency.

202
00:24:12,876 --> 00:24:13,237
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

203
00:24:15,288 --> 00:24:19,914
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's good to have a sense of urgency and you need to have it.

204
00:24:20,014 --> 00:24:26,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you won't be going against the grain, as the Buddha said.

205
00:24:26,682 --> 00:24:29,385
[SPEAKER_01]: But it needs to be informed.

206
00:24:30,387 --> 00:24:31,488
[SPEAKER_01]: It needs to be redirected.

207
00:24:41,645 --> 00:24:51,368
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, yeah, if I feel impatient and I feel like I'm not going fast enough with my practice, that's fine.

208
00:24:51,388 --> 00:24:57,670
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean you need to kind of, like, deny it and say, nah, you know, it's good enough or something.

209
00:24:58,090 --> 00:25:07,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, no, maybe you are lacking in some respects, but you have to keep in mind, like,

210
00:25:09,144 --> 00:25:21,572
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't even necessarily... like, you're not gonna get anything of value by analyzing things in this kind of abstract way.

211
00:25:21,632 --> 00:25:26,836
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, kind of, as if there is some specific action that I just have to stop.

212
00:25:27,930 --> 00:25:31,671
[SPEAKER_01]: There's some specific behavior that I just have to say no to forever.

213
00:25:31,691 --> 00:25:35,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Except the precepts, of course, but we're assuming that that's covered.

214
00:25:37,332 --> 00:25:47,815
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have to be ready that, no, it's really just I have to be vigilant for completely new things that I've never encountered before.

215
00:25:50,136 --> 00:25:54,717
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's going to be more accurate than thinking like, oh, it's just these

216
00:25:56,475 --> 00:26:02,039
[SPEAKER_01]: I can just make a determination and then assume that everything that comes after is going to be covered.

217
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:23,072
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're like... you're facing each day and you're making... you're making your decisions based on what impulses have I... am I discerning right now.

218
00:26:23,092 --> 00:26:24,353
[SPEAKER_00]: Is the sense of urgency

219
00:26:26,037 --> 00:26:31,659
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's problematic because it comes with a pressure that you can't endure.

220
00:26:32,919 --> 00:26:33,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

221
00:26:33,540 --> 00:26:35,320
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the sense of urgency that's problematic.

222
00:26:35,380 --> 00:26:43,603
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you need to have it in order to practice, even the craving.

223
00:26:43,903 --> 00:26:47,084
[SPEAKER_01]: But as I said, craving is overcome by means of craving.

224
00:26:49,125 --> 00:26:54,287
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is going to be a pressure behind it.

225
00:26:55,782 --> 00:27:00,004
[SPEAKER_01]: But, like I said, it matters what you do on account of that pressure.

226
00:27:00,064 --> 00:27:01,245
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not all on the same level.

227
00:27:03,406 --> 00:27:16,792
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can have a sense of urgency and you can decide to do 1000 prostrations to diminish your conceit.

228
00:27:18,332 --> 00:27:22,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, that's a very coarse example, obviously, but just to illustrate the principle.

229
00:27:24,274 --> 00:27:31,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you can look more closely at when you speak, why are you speaking?

230
00:27:32,676 --> 00:27:34,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, what are your motivations for speech?

231
00:27:37,297 --> 00:27:43,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, oh, I should speak less, automatically, and I should just take a vow of silence.

232
00:27:47,718 --> 00:28:04,291
[SPEAKER_00]: The pressure of the sense of urgency, is that different from, you know, you're just taking on the precepts and now you're calming down within, you know, that pressure that you get when you first take on the precepts and it's like... Obviously, then you just want to put out the fire.

233
00:28:04,852 --> 00:28:08,455
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the sense of urgency is what would have led you to take on the precepts.

234
00:28:08,935 --> 00:28:12,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you didn't feel like you were

235
00:28:13,979 --> 00:28:18,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Under some kind of threat, you wouldn't have done such an unnatural thing.

236
00:28:22,945 --> 00:28:25,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Which was certainly, at least in the beginning, it wasn't natural.

237
00:28:28,528 --> 00:28:34,913
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not even like... For example, see, you could even say, oh, okay, so I shouldn't have a sense of urgency.

238
00:28:37,314 --> 00:28:42,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, again, it's like you remove or you try to remove

239
00:28:45,245 --> 00:28:58,552
[SPEAKER_01]: The opportunity for problems to arise, rather than just removing the problems, which is always going to fail, because as long as you exist, there is going to be the opportunity for problems, for defilements to arise.

240
00:28:59,613 --> 00:29:01,674
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sense of urgency isn't the issue.

241
00:29:03,055 --> 00:29:11,142
[SPEAKER_01]: It's obviously something that can result in kind of misdirections, but it's not the issue in and of itself.

242
00:29:12,183 --> 00:29:17,847
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically the fact that when you have the sense of urgency, it comes up.

243
00:29:20,269 --> 00:29:27,115
[SPEAKER_01]: You let it overly proliferate into just a blind restlessness.

244
00:29:31,632 --> 00:29:33,722
[SPEAKER_01]: But as a... as a... just a call to action.

245
00:29:35,786 --> 00:29:39,788
[SPEAKER_01]: It could very well be, oh, actually, I should restrain my restlessness.

246
00:29:39,868 --> 00:29:41,790
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will be doing that out of a sense of urgency.

247
00:29:43,190 --> 00:29:46,372
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could actually realize, okay, I need to practice.

248
00:29:48,013 --> 00:29:48,794
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not doing enough.

249
00:29:50,015 --> 00:29:55,338
[SPEAKER_01]: And then realize, oh, actually, that's true.

250
00:29:55,398 --> 00:29:56,338
[SPEAKER_01]: I haven't been doing enough.

251
00:29:56,398 --> 00:30:01,842
[SPEAKER_01]: But right now, I have this this pressure to, I don't know,

252
00:30:04,448 --> 00:30:06,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Just start saying no to something.

253
00:30:07,891 --> 00:30:09,852
[SPEAKER_01]: That I'm... that is not even always unwholesome.

254
00:30:11,233 --> 00:30:20,319
[SPEAKER_01]: But like, I just feel that if I just remove this, I'll be kind of somehow getting ahead.

255
00:30:21,820 --> 00:30:26,063
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, well, but this is clearly coming out of that impulse.

256
00:30:27,624 --> 00:30:32,087
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I have a sense of urgency that I should be freeing myself from craving.

257
00:30:33,816 --> 00:30:34,617
[SPEAKER_01]: That's right.

258
00:30:36,138 --> 00:30:36,738
[SPEAKER_01]: It's right there.

259
00:30:39,420 --> 00:30:44,885
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you will need the sense of urgency to stick to that, because it's not going to happen automatically.

260
00:30:45,005 --> 00:30:49,788
[SPEAKER_01]: You let your guard down, and then something else is going to come up, and you will act out of it.

261
00:30:50,649 --> 00:30:57,554
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the whole thing of like, oh, you abstain from these

262
00:31:00,043 --> 00:31:09,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Restless activities, activities based on craving, many times, can sound to people as if, okay, so, that just means you don't do anything, basically.

263
00:31:09,590 --> 00:31:15,894
[SPEAKER_01]: But the thing is, or there's no need for effort.

264
00:31:15,994 --> 00:31:18,776
[SPEAKER_02]: It's all just passive.

265
00:31:18,956 --> 00:31:20,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

266
00:31:20,597 --> 00:31:24,780
[SPEAKER_01]: But, I mean, this is to describe it all the time.

267
00:31:25,761 --> 00:31:26,942
[SPEAKER_01]: It will take effort.

268
00:31:28,130 --> 00:31:43,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, see, if it was easier to stick to that middle way and neither indulge nor go for these blind observances, then you wouldn't need the Buddha.

269
00:31:45,179 --> 00:31:49,741
[SPEAKER_01]: It would just be natural for everybody to already be doing it.

270
00:31:50,181 --> 00:31:54,844
[SPEAKER_01]: But you will need effort to pull yourself back from both of those directions.

271
00:31:55,784 --> 00:31:57,305
[SPEAKER_01]: And actually, you don't need effort.

272
00:31:59,092 --> 00:32:03,374
[SPEAKER_01]: of that right kind to go for one of the two, because it's what your mind wants anyway.

273
00:32:03,794 --> 00:32:06,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Like as I said, those impulses, you kind of find them there already.

274
00:32:09,557 --> 00:32:18,882
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if it feels like effort, like when, let's say, when you really want to do something, when there's a project that you really want to complete,

275
00:32:20,331 --> 00:32:23,713
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you spend days and nights, like, not sleeping or whatever.

276
00:32:25,134 --> 00:32:29,477
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you might think, okay, yeah, this requires effort.

277
00:32:29,897 --> 00:32:31,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, sure, in a worldly sense.

278
00:32:31,318 --> 00:32:41,124
[SPEAKER_01]: But the effort of the practice, the effort that leads to awakening, is like when it's not actually something that you have an impulse for.

279
00:32:43,666 --> 00:32:45,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Because otherwise, it cannot be nibbāna.

280
00:32:48,708 --> 00:32:50,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Nibbāna is the cessation of impulses.

281
00:32:51,915 --> 00:32:59,171
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't arrive at that magically, by giving into impulses.

